# /evaluate was the security review pack. It folded into /security, which now
# carries every claim it made. The sitemap had already published /evaluate/ to
# crawlers, so the route has real inbound links even pre-launch.
/evaluate /security/ 308
/evaluate/ /security/ 308
# /demo was the scenario page. It folded into the homepage, and the section it
# folded into — four scenarios that ran the SDK into a failure on purpose — was
# cut from that page later. What survives under this name is the live demo, so
# the target is the exhibit inside `DemoConsole.astro`: the fragment is on the
# animation rather than on the paragraph above it, which is argued where the id
# is written.
#
# The target carries a fragment because Cloudflare's redirects support them in
# the destination ("Can include fragments, query strings, splats, and
# placeholders"), and only a line-leading # is a comment. The sitemap published
# /demo/ too.
#
# The cost, recorded because it is a real one: a destination fragment replaces
# whatever fragment the reader arrived with, so /demo#flip-a-byte lands on the
# demo rather than on a scenario. It would now land nowhere in any case — the
# scenario ids left with the section — and bare /demo is what the sitemap, the
# old nav item, and any bookmark all point at.
/demo /#demo 308
/demo/ /#demo 308
# /compare was the comparison page. Its table and its five project notes are now
# the two bands under "How it compares" on /product, so the destination carries
# that section's id.
#
# The fragment costs less here than it did for /demo: /compare published no deep
# links of its own — it had no ids on any of its four sections — so nothing that
# arrives with a fragment can have it replaced. The sitemap had already given
# /compare/ to crawlers, and it was a nav item on every page of the site, which
# is the widest inbound surface any route here has had.
/compare /product/#how-it-compares 308
/compare/ /product/#how-it-compares 308
